 7. 🏈 Senate passes bill to rein in college sports payments and transfers 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Sen. Ted Cruz (R-Texas) promotes the Protect College Sports Act at the Capitol on Sept. 14. Photo: Tom Williams/CQ-Roll Call via Getty Images Last night's Senate passage of the Protect College Sports Act, by an overwhelming 77-22, is the government's biggest move yet to set national rules for a college sports system transformed by athlete pay, lawsuits and competing state rules, Axios' Kate Hunter writes. 

 The legislation gives the NCAA new authority over payments to college athletes for their name, image and likeness (NIL) and how often they can transfer, and limited antitrust protections to enforce those rules. President Trump praised the bill in a late-night post. 🏛️ What to watch: The bill faces an uncertain path in the House. If it stalls, the future of college sports will remain in the hands of courts, states, schools and athletic governing bodies.

 
 
